## 4. The Interaction Model

### The Write → Compile → Inspect Cycle

```
╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
║                    USER INTERACTION LOOP                      ║
║                                                               ║
║   ┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐     ║
║   │  1. WRITE FREELY                                    │     ║
║   │     User types specs in natural language.           │     ║
║   │     No vocabulary restrictions.                     │     ║
║   │     Can write one sentence or a full paragraph.     │     ║
║   │     Can specify which layer they're targeting       │     ║
║   │     or let the system infer it.                     │     ║
║   └──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘     ║
║                          │                                     ║
║                    [ COMPILE BUTTON ]                          ║
║                    User explicitly triggers                    ║
║                          │                                     ║
║   ┌──────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐     ║
║   │  2. GRAPH GENERATION (automatic)                    │     ║
║   │     AI agent processes free-form text:              │     ║
║   │     • Extracts atomic requirements → new nodes      │     ║
║   │     • Classifies each node into a formal layer      │     ║
║   │     • Infers dependency edges to existing nodes     │     ║
║   │     • Assigns to families (new or existing)         │     ║
║   │     • Runs conflict detection against full graph    │     ║
║   └──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘     ║
║                          │                                     ║
║   ┌──────────────────────▼──────────────────────────────┐     ║
║   │  3. FEEDBACK DISPLAY (immediate)                    │     ║
║   │     System shows:                                   │     ║
║   │     • New nodes created (with layer assignments)    │     ║
║   │     • Edges inferred                                │     ║
║   │     • 🔴 CONFLICTS highlighted (no fix suggested    │     ║
║   │          unless user asks)                          │     ║
║   │     • 🟡 AMBIGUITIES flagged (possible multiple     │     ║
║   │          interpretations noted)                     │     ║
║   │     • 🟢 VALIDATED nodes (consistent with graph)    │     ║
║   └──────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────┘     ║
║                          │                                     ║
║                  User decides next action:                     ║
║                  • Write more specs → loop back to 1           ║
║                  • Fix conflicts → loop back to 1              ║
║                  • Ask for help → system suggests fixes        ║
║                  • Trigger code generation → exit to codegen   ║
║                                                               ║
╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
```

### The Conflict Highlighting Philosophy

This deserves emphasis because it encodes an important UX principle:

```
CONFLICT DETECTION RULES:

✅ DO: Highlight the conflicting nodes immediately
✅ DO: Show WHY they conflict (which constraints are violated)
✅ DO: Show the dependency path that creates the conflict

❌ DON'T: Auto-suggest fixes
❌ DON'T: Block the user from continuing
❌ DON'T: Require resolution before adding more specs

RATIONALE:
• User may have context the system doesn't
• User may be mid-thought and doesn't want interruption
• Premature fix suggestions can anchor thinking in wrong direction
• User explicitly asks for help when ready

EXCEPTION:
• If user presses a "Help me resolve" button on a specific
  conflict, THEN the system provides fix suggestions
```

