## 7. Prior Art & Similar Approaches

### Category A: Requirements Management Tools (Closest Lineage)

| Tool | Similarity | Key Difference |
|------|-----------|----------------|
| **IBM DOORS / DOORS Next** | Graph-based requirements traceability, layers of requirements linked by dependencies | No AI processing, no free-form input, very manual node creation. Heavy enterprise tool. No code generation. |
| **Jama Connect** | Requirements traceability matrices, impact analysis when requirements change, collaborative | No AI, no conflict auto-detection, no layered graph model |
| **Polarion (Siemens)** | Full ALM with requirements linked to tests linked to code | Enterprise-grade but no free-form-to-graph pipeline |
| **Reqtify (Dassault Systèmes)** | Traceability across requirement documents, impact analysis | Focused on document linking, not graph construction |

**Your idea differs from all of these because**: they require humans to manually create and link requirements in structured forms. Your idea lets humans write freely and the AI builds the graph.

---

### Category B: Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE)

| Tool/Method | Similarity | Key Difference |
|-------------|-----------|----------------|
| **SysML / Capella** | Formal layered models: requirements → functional → logical → physical architecture | Visual modeling language, requires expertise, no NL input |
| **Enterprise Architect (Sparx)** | UML/SysML modeling with traceability | Manual model creation, no AI, steep learning curve |
| **ARCADIA Method** | Formal layers: operational → system → logical → physical | Method for systems engineering, not software spec-to-code |

**Your idea differs because**: MBSE requires learning a formal modeling language. Your approach accepts natural language and infers the formal structure.

---

### Category C: AI-Powered Spec-to-Code (Modern)

| Tool | Similarity | Key Difference |
|------|-----------|----------------|
| **MetaGPT** | Multi-agent framework that generates PRD → Design Doc → Tasks → Code. Has formal layers. | Fully automated pipeline — human doesn't interactively build the graph. No conflict detection. No collaboration model. |
| **GPT Engineer** | Takes a spec prompt, asks clarifying questions, generates code | Single-pass, no persistent graph, no layered architecture, no multi-user |
| **Sweep AI** | Converts GitHub issues to PRs with code | Issue-level granularity, no requirement decomposition |
| **Factory AI / Devin** | AI agent that plans and writes code from descriptions | Agent-oriented, no visible spec graph, no human-in-the-loop graph building |
| **Cursor / Windsurf** | IDE with AI that takes specs in context files | No graph structure, no conflict detection, single-user mental model |

**Your idea differs because**: these tools treat specification as an input to be consumed once, not as a living, growing, validated graph that multiple people build incrementally.

---

### Category D: Formal Specification & Constraint Systems

| Method/Tool | Similarity | Key Difference |
|-------------|-----------|----------------|
| **Alloy Analyzer** | Builds a model, automatically finds constraint violations and counter-examples | Requires formal language (Alloy), not natural language |
| **TLA+ / PlusCal** | Formal specification with model checking for conflict/invariant violation detection | Requires mathematical specification language |
| **Specification by Example (Gojko Adzic)** | Collaborative specification through concrete examples, bridges business and technical | Method, not a tool. No graph, no AI, no automation |
| **Event Storming (Alberto Brandolini)** | Collaborative domain discovery using sticky notes, natural grouping into aggregates | Workshop method. Physical sticky notes on wall. Your idea is essentially a **digital, AI-powered Event Storming** with persistent graph structure |

---

### Category E: Build Systems (Your Explicit Analogy)

| Tool | What you're borrowing |
|------|----------------------|
| **CMake** | Dependency graph construction before compilation, target-level nodes |
| **Make** | Directed acyclic graph of dependencies, incremental rebuilding |
| **Bazel** | Hermetic builds, dependency graph with strict layering, visibility rules between targets |
| **Gradle** | Task dependency graph, configuration phases before execution |
| **Nx (Monorepo)** | Project graph with affected-target detection — knows what to rebuild when something changes |

**The borrowed principle**: separate the **graph construction phase** (understanding dependencies) from the **execution phase** (compilation/generation). Your SDD-Compiler does exactly this for specifications.

---

### The Closest Known Analog

The single closest thing to your idea is probably a combination of:

> **MetaGPT's layered agent pipeline**
> \+
> **IBM DOORS' traceability graph**
> \+
> **Event Storming's collaborative discovery**
> \+
> **Alloy's automatic conflict detection**
> \+
> **CMake's dependency graph model**

**No existing product combines all five of these characteristics.** That is where your idea lives — in the intersection.

