## 7. Visualization Strategy

```python
# viz.py — Core visualization logic

"""
LAYOUT STRATEGY:

Y-axis = Layer (L0 at top, L6 at bottom)
X-axis = Spread nodes horizontally within each layer

    Y=0  ── L0: Business ──     [B1]  [B2]  [B3]
    Y=1  ── L1: Functional ──   [F1]  [F2]  [F3]  [F4]
    Y=2  ── L2: Architecture ── [A1]  [A2]
    Y=3  ── L3: Interfaces ──   [I1]  [I2]  [I3]
    Y=4  ── L4: Data Models ──  [D1]  [D2]
    Y=5  ── L5: Logic ──        [L1]  [L2]  [L3]
    Y=6  ── L6: Tests ──        [T1]  [T2]  [T3]  [T4]

COLOR SCHEME:
    validated  → #4CAF50 (green)
    conflict   → #F44336 (red)
    ambiguous  → #FF9800 (yellow/orange)
    new        → #2196F3 (blue)
    draft      → #9E9E9E (grey)

EDGE STYLES:
    depends_on → solid grey arrow
    derives    → solid blue arrow
    conflicts  → dashed red line (no arrow, bidirectional)

NODE SHAPE:
    All nodes are circles or boxes with label = node ID
    Hover tooltip shows full content text

FAMILY INDICATION:
    Nodes in same family have a subtle shared background
    or border color
"""
```

### Pyvis vs Streamlit-Agraph Decision

```
PYVIS (recommended):
  ✅ Interactive (drag, zoom, hover, click)
  ✅ Physics-based layout (can override with fixed positions)
  ✅ Renders as HTML embedded in Streamlit via st.components
  ✅ Hover tooltips built-in
  ✅ Edge styling (dashed, colored, arrows)
  ❌ Requires HTML component embedding (minor friction)

STREAMLIT-AGRAPH:
  ✅ Native Streamlit component
  ✅ Click events return node ID to Streamlit
  ✅ Simpler API
  ❌ Less visual customization
  ❌ Harder to control layer-based layout

RECOMMENDATION: Use streamlit-agraph for tighter Streamlit
integration and click-event handling. Fall back to Pyvis
if visual quality is insufficient.
```

