            border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
            margin: 48px 0;
        }

        /* TOC */
        .toc {
            background: var(--bg-secondary);
            border: 1px solid var(--border-color);
            border-radius: 12px;
            padding: 32px;
            margin-bottom: 48px;
        }

        .toc h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
            color: var(--text-primary);
        }

        .toc ol {
            color: var(--text-secondary);
        }

        .toc a {
            color: var(--accent-blue);
            text-decoration: none;
        }

        .toc a:hover {
            text-decoration: underline;
        }

        /* Emoji Status */
        .status-green { color: var(--accent-green); }
        .status-red { color: var(--accent-red); }
        .status-yellow { color: var(--accent-yellow); }

        /* Footer */
        .footer {
            text-align: center;
            padding: 40px 0;
            border-top: 1px solid var(--border-color);
            margin-top: 60px;
            color: var(--text-muted);
            font-size: 0.9em;
        }

        /* Responsive */
        @media (max-width: 768px) {
            .hero h1 { font-size: 2em; }
            .comparison { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
            .card-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
            .layer-item { flex-direction: column; }
            .layer-label { border-radius: 8px 8px 0 0; min-width: auto; }
            .layer-content { border-left: 1px solid var(--border-color); border-radius: 0 0 8px 8px; }
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="container">

    <!-- ==================== HERO ==================== -->
    <div class="hero">
        <h1>SDD-Compiler</h1>
        <div class="subtitle">A Product Spec Compiler &amp; IDE</div>
        <div class="tagline">
            "Don't make humans write better specifications. Build a system that asks better questions —
            grounded in product knowledge and validated by executable evidence — until the specification
            emerges as a byproduct of the conversation."
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ==================== TOC ==================== -->
    <div class="toc">
        <h3>Contents</h3>
        <ol>
            <li><a href="#sdd-framework">What is Spec-Driven Development?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#death-by-markdown">The "Death by Markdown" Problem</a></li>
            <li><a href="#vocabulary-trap">The Vocabulary Spectrum Trap</a></li>
            <li><a href="#prior-art">Prior Attempts at Strict Specifications</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reframing">Reframing the Problem</a></li>
            <li><a href="#architecture">SDD-Compiler Architecture</a></li>
            <li><a href="#formal-layers">The Formal Layers for Code Generation</a></li>
            <li><a href="#interaction-model">Interaction Model &amp; Conflict Philosophy</a></li>
            <li><a href="#ai-internals">How AI Agents Actually Process Spec Comparison</a></li>
            <li><a href="#graph-value">Does the Graph Actually Help?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#similar-work">Prior Art &amp; Similar Products</a></li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 1: SDD ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="sdd-framework">
        <div class="section-number">Section 01</div>
        <h2>What is Spec-Driven Development?</h2>

        <p>
            Spec-Driven Development (SDD) is an emerging software development paradigm that has gained
            significant traction alongside AI-powered code generation tools. At its core, SDD inverts
            the traditional developer workflow.
        </p>

        <div class="comparison">
            <div class="compare-box">
                <h4>Traditional Development</h4>
                <p>Think → Code → Test → Refine</p>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box positive">
                <h4>Spec-Driven Development</h4>
                <p>Think → <strong>Specify</strong> → Generate → Validate</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p>
            In SDD, the <strong>specification becomes the primary artifact</strong>, not the code.
            The developer's job shifts from <em>writing code</em> to <em>writing precise, unambiguous
            specifications</em> that an AI system can consume to produce correct, working code.
        </p>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p><strong>Code generation is becoming commoditized. Intent expression is not.</strong></p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Why SDD Has Gained Wide Adoption</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Driver</th><th>Explanation</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>AI code quality improved</td><td>LLMs now generate production-quality code for many common patterns</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Speed</td><td>Generating code from a spec is vastly faster than writing it by hand</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Consistency</td><td>AI-generated code follows style guides and patterns uniformly</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Non-traditional developers (PMs, designers) can participate more directly</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Cost reduction</td><td>Less developer time per feature means lower cost per unit of output</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>The SDD Ecosystem in Practice</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Markdown files</strong> (<code>.md</code>) as the primary specification format</li>
            <li><strong>Prompt templates</strong> that structure how specs are fed to AI</li>
            <li><strong>Context files</strong> (like <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>cursor_rules</code>) that give AI agents persistent project knowledge</li>
            <li><strong>Review cycles</strong> where humans validate AI output against the spec</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 2: PROBLEM ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="death-by-markdown">
        <div class="section-number">Section 02</div>
        <h2>The "Death by Markdown" Problem</h2>

        <p>
            SDD was supposed to solve the bottleneck of code production. And it did. But SDD introduced
            a <strong>new bottleneck</strong>: specification production. Writing a good spec is hard — it
            requires deep product understanding, anticipation of edge cases, awareness of system constraints,
            and clear, unambiguous language.
        </p>

        <h3>The "AI Writing Specs" Escape Attempt</h3>

        <p>
            Faced with this difficulty, teams naturally asked: <em>"If AI can write code from specs,
            why can't AI also write the specs?"</em> So people started using AI to generate the
            specifications themselves. This creates a compounding problem:
        </p>

        <pre>
Vague Human Intent
       ↓
  AI-Generated Spec (Markdown)     ← May not match true intent
       ↓
  AI-Generated Code                ← Faithful to spec, but spec may be wrong
       ↓
  Output that looks right but may be subtly wrong</pre>

        <p>
            The AI-generated spec <strong>appears</strong> thorough — well-formatted, comprehensive-looking,
            using all the right terminology. But it may hallucinate requirements, miss critical edge cases,
            contradict existing system behavior, or use ambiguous language.
        </p>

        <h3>The Shifted Bottleneck</h3>

        <blockquote class="critical">
            <p>
                Instead of reviewing code to check if it's correct, developers must now
                <strong>review massive markdown documents</strong> to check if the <em>specification</em>
                is correct. The bottleneck didn't disappear. It multiplied.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Problem</th><th>Explanation</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Spec review is cognitively expensive</td><td>Reading a 20-page spec and verifying it matches your vague mental model is exhausting</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Specs are deceptively readable</td><td>AI-generated markdown <em>reads</em> well, making you <em>feel</em> like it's correct even when it isn't</td></tr>
                <tr><td>No compiler for specs</td><td>Code has compilers, linters, type-checkers, and tests. Specs have human eyeballs.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Specs lack execution feedback</td><td>You can run code and see if it works. You can't "run" a spec.</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Compounding errors</td><td>If you miss a spec error, it propagates into code, tests, and docs — all consistently wrong</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>The Problem Stack</h3>

        <div class="problem-stack">
            <div class="problem-level">
                <div class="level-header">
                    <span class="level-tag tag-problem">Problem 1</span>
                    <span class="level-tag tag-solution">Solved</span>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Code production was the bottleneck.</strong><br>
                Solution: SDD + AI code generation. Result: Code production is now cheap &amp; fast.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="problem-level">
                <div class="level-header">
                    <span class="level-tag tag-problem">Problem 2</span>
                    <span class="level-tag tag-result">Attempted</span>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Writing good specs is hard.</strong><br>
                Solution: Use AI to generate specs too. Result: Specs are produced fast but may be wrong in subtle, hard-to-detect ways.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="problem-level">
                <div class="level-header">
                    <span class="level-tag tag-problem">Problem 3</span>
                    <span class="level-tag tag-unknown">Unsolved</span>
                </div>
                <p><strong>Now we must review specs AND code.</strong><br>
                "Death by Markdown" — shifted bottleneck, not solved it. This is what SDD-Compiler proposes to address.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="problem-level" style="border: 1px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <div class="level-header">
                    <span class="level-tag tag-problem">Root Cause</span>
                </div>
                <p><strong>There is no "compiler" for specs.</strong> No automated system validates whether a specification actually matches human intent, is internally consistent, and aligns with the existing product's reality.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 3: VOCABULARY TRAP ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="vocabulary-trap">
        <div class="section-number">Section 03</div>
        <h2>The Vocabulary Spectrum Trap</h2>

        <p>
            SDD specs are vague and free-form by nature. Unlike code, which has strict vocabulary
            (<code>if</code>, <code>else</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, function names),
            natural language specifications are inherently ambiguous. But if we enforce strict vocabulary
            on specifications, we inevitably slide toward creating another programming language —
            defeating the entire purpose of SDD.
        </p>

        <div class="spectrum">
            <span class="spectrum-label spectrum-left">
                VAGUE<br>
                Natural Language<br>
                ✅ Easy to write<br>
                ❌ Ambiguous
            </span>
            <span class="spectrum-label spectrum-center">
                ???<br>
                Constrained NL<br>
                The sweet spot?
            </span>
            <span class="spectrum-label spectrum-right">
                PRECISE<br>
                Programming Language<br>
                ✅ Unambiguous<br>
                ❌ Hard to write
            </span>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="warning">
            <p>
                <strong>The Vocabulary Trap:</strong> Any attempt to make specifications precise enough
                for machines to compile will, if taken to its logical conclusion, converge toward a
                programming language — thereby recreating the exact problem SDD was designed to solve.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 4: PRIOR ART ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="prior-art">
        <div class="section-number">Section 04</div>
        <h2>Prior Attempts at Strict Specifications</h2>

        <p>
            The vocabulary trap has been sprung many times before. Studying these attempts reveals
            what works, what fails, and why.
        </p>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <div class="card">
                <h4>Formal Specification Languages <span style="color:var(--text-muted);">(1970s–2000s)</span></h4>
                <p>Z Notation, VDM, TLA+, Alloy, B Method — mathematically rigorous specification languages.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px;"><span class="status-red">Verdict:</span> Extremely precise but almost nobody used them outside academia. Became a programming language for mathematicians.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>UML / Model-Driven Architecture <span style="color:var(--text-muted);">(1990s–2010s)</span></h4>
                <p>Visual specification language with the dream of generating code directly from diagrams.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px;"><span class="status-red">Verdict:</span> Shifted complexity from text to diagrams. Did not reduce it. Diagram-code sync was a nightmare.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>BDD / Gherkin Syntax <span style="color:var(--text-muted);">(2000s–present)</span></h4>
                <p>Constrained natural language (<code>Given/When/Then</code>) for writing executable specifications.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px;"><span class="status-yellow">Verdict:</span> Most successful attempt. Found a useful niche but couldn't generalize to full product specification.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>Design by Contract — Eiffel <span style="color:var(--text-muted);">(1986–present)</span></h4>
                <p>Embedded specifications (preconditions, postconditions, invariants) directly into code.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px;"><span class="status-yellow">Verdict:</span> Made code more self-documenting but didn't help non-coders specify intent.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>Structured Prompt Engineering <span style="color:var(--text-muted);">(2023–present)</span></h4>
                <p>Frameworks like CRISP, RACE, CO-STAR, LMQL, Guidance, BAML — systematizing how prompts are structured.</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px;"><span class="status-yellow">Verdict:</span> Useful tools, wrong level of abstraction. They optimize the AI interface, not the human intent discovery process.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p>
                <strong>The Key Lesson:</strong> No fixed point on the vocabulary spectrum works for all
                situations. The right level of precision depends on the context, the domain, the team,
                and the specific requirement being expressed.
            </p>
        </blockquote>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 5: REFRAMING ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="reframing">
        <div class="section-number">Section 05</div>
        <h2>Reframing the Problem</h2>

        <p>
            The word "compiler" implies well-defined input syntax. But we just established that enforcing
            well-defined syntax leads to the vocabulary trap. So the metaphor of a compiler may be misleading.
            What is the <strong>fundamental problem</strong>?
        </p>

        <blockquote>
            <p>
                A human has a vague, incomplete, partially contradictory mental model of what they want.
                We need a process that converges this fuzzy intent into a precise, validated,
                implementation-ready specification — without requiring the human to learn a formal language.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Fundamental Requirements</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Description</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>R1: Intent Discovery</strong></td><td>Humans don't fully know what they want. The system must help them <em>discover</em> their intent, not just document it.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>R2: Ambiguity Resolution</strong></td><td>Natural language is inherently ambiguous. The system must detect ambiguity and resolve it through interaction, not vocabulary enforcement.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>R3: Consistency Validation</strong></td><td>New specs must not contradict existing product behavior, previous decisions, or each other.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>R4: Progressive Formalization</strong></td><td>Start vague, get precise incrementally. Don't demand precision upfront.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>R5: Executable Feedback</strong></td><td>The only way to truly validate a spec is to see its consequences — generated tests, prototypes, simulations.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>R6: Learning / Memory</strong></td><td>Each completed cycle should make the system smarter about this specific product.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Better Metaphors Than "Compiler"</h3>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <div class="card">
                <h4>🧑‍💻 Senior Engineer Pair Partner</h4>
                <p>Asks probing questions, points out contradictions, suggests patterns, sketches prototypes, and remembers everything. The specification <strong>emerges from dialogue</strong>.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>🔍 Type Inference Engine</h4>
                <p>Let humans express intent loosely. <strong>Infer</strong> detailed requirements from context. <strong>Flag</strong> when inferences conflict. <strong>Ask</strong> the human to disambiguate only when necessary.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card">
                <h4>🧪 Test-Driven Spec</h4>
                <p>Instead of abstract requirements, provide <strong>concrete examples</strong>. The system generalizes, generates counter-examples, and you confirm or deny — progressively refining the spec.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Reframed Technical Problem</h3>

        <pre>
Build a system that:

1. ACCEPTS vague natural language input
   (no strict vocabulary required)

2. CONVERGES toward precise specifications through
   multi-turn interaction (not single-pass compilation)

3. GROUNDS every specification element in something
   verifiable: a test case, a code sketch, a counter-example,
   or a reference to existing product behavior

4. VALIDATES consistency against a growing knowledge
   base of the product's history, decisions, and constraints

5. LEARNS from each completed cycle to become more
   effective at intent discovery for THIS product

The output is NOT a markdown document.
The output is a VALIDATED SPECIFICATION GRAPH:
a structured, interconnected, tested set of
requirements with full traceability.</pre>

        <h3>Three Fundamental Technical Pillars</h3>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <h4>Pillar 1: Interactive Convergence Engine</h4>
                <p>How do you systematically narrow vague intent into precise specification through dialogue? What questions should be asked and in what order? When is a specification "converged enough"?</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px; color: var(--text-muted); font-size:0.85em;">Territory: dialogue systems, active learning, information-theoretic question selection</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-yellow);">
                <h4>Pillar 2: Grounding &amp; Validation Mechanism</h4>
                <p>How do you check that a spec element is meaningful and consistent? Can you generate executable artifacts from partial specs to provide feedback? Can you detect conflicts with existing product state?</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px; color: var(--text-muted); font-size:0.85em;">Territory: constraint satisfaction, knowledge graphs, code/test generation</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-green);">
                <h4>Pillar 3: Product-Specific Learning Loop</h4>
                <p>How does the system accumulate knowledge about THIS product over time? How do real-world outcomes feed back? How do you represent product knowledge for retrieval and reasoning?</p>
                <p style="margin-top:8px; color: var(--text-muted); font-size:0.85em;">Territory: RAG, episodic memory, knowledge graphs, fine-tuning</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 6: ARCHITECTURE ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="architecture">
        <div class="section-number">Section 06</div>
        <h2>SDD-Compiler Architecture</h2>

        <h3>Core Concept: The Specification Graph</h3>

        <p>
            Just as <code>CMake</code> parses source files and builds a <strong>dependency graph</strong>
            of targets, sources, and build rules before any compilation happens, the SDD-Compiler parses
            free-form human input and builds a <strong>specification graph</strong> of requirements,
            dependencies, and constraints before any code generation happens.
        </p>

        <div class="flow">
            <div class="flow-step">CMakeLists.txt</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">cmake</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">Dependency Graph</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">make</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">Compiled Binary</div>
        </div>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="flow-step" style="border-color: var(--accent-blue);">Free-form Spec</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step" style="border-color: var(--accent-blue);">SDD-Compiler</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step" style="border-color: var(--accent-blue);">Spec Graph</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step" style="border-color: var(--accent-blue);">AI Agent</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step" style="border-color: var(--accent-blue);">Generated Code</div>
        </div>

        <h3>Graph Structure</h3>

        <pre>
SPECIFICATION GRAPH (Directed Acyclic Graph)

  Nodes  = Individual atomic requirements
  Edges  = Dependencies, derivations, and constraints between requirements
  Layers = Formal strata that organize nodes by abstraction level

  Properties of each node:
  ┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
  │  id:          unique identifier     │
  │  content:     the requirement text  │
  │  layer:       which formal layer    │
  │  status:      draft|validated|conflict│
  │  author:      who wrote it          │
  │  source_text: original free-form    │
  │  depends_on:  [node_ids]            │
  │  conflicts:   [node_ids]            │
  │  family:      grouping cluster      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────┘</pre>

        <h3>Dual-View Architecture</h3>

        <p>The graph serves two fundamentally different consumers:</p>

        <div class="comparison">
            <div class="compare-box">
                <h4>👤 Human View (Collapsed)</h4>
                <p>Nodes grouped into <strong>families</strong> — high-level clusters that hide individual node complexity. Humans see summaries like "Password Reset (12 nodes: 10 ✅, 2 ❌)" and can drill down on demand.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box">
                <h4>🤖 Agent View (Expanded)</h4>
                <p>Every individual node with full dependency edges, conflict markers, and traceability links. AI agents consume this for code generation, conflict detection, and test synthesis.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h4>Family Grouping Criteria (in priority order)</h4>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Functional Cohesion</strong> — nodes describing the same feature/capability</li>
            <li><strong>Layer Proximity</strong> — nodes in the same formal layer</li>
            <li><strong>Dependency Clusters</strong> — nodes that heavily depend on each other</li>
            <li><strong>Author Proximity</strong> — nodes written by the same person in the same session</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 7: FORMAL LAYERS ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="formal-layers">
        <div class="section-number">Section 07</div>
        <h2>The Formal Layers for Code Generation</h2>

        <p>
            Each layer <strong>constrains</strong> the layers below it — exactly analogous to how
            in a build system, a library target constrains the compilation of binaries that depend on it.
        </p>

        <div class="layer-stack">
            <div class="layer-item l0">
                <div class="layer-label">L0 — Business</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>WHY</strong> does this exist? What value does it deliver?<br>
                    <em>Example: "Users must reset passwords without contacting support"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l1">
                <div class="layer-label">L1 — Functional</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>WHAT</strong> exactly should the system do?<br>
                    <em>Example: "User clicks 'forgot password' → receives email within 30s"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l2">
                <div class="layer-label">L2 — Architecture</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>HOW</strong> is the system structured at component level?<br>
                    <em>Example: "Password reset handled by AuthService microservice"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l3">
                <div class="layer-label">L3 — Interfaces</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>HOW</strong> do components communicate?<br>
                    <em>Example: "POST /auth/reset-password {email: string} → 202 Accepted"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l4">
                <div class="layer-label">L4 — Data Models</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>WHAT</strong> data exists and how is it structured?<br>
                    <em>Example: "PasswordResetToken {id, userId, token, expiresAt, usedAt}"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l5">
                <div class="layer-label">L5 — Logic</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>HOW</strong> does the internal logic work?<br>
                    <em>Example: "On reset request: if failedAttempts ≥ 3 AND now < lockedUntil → reject"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="layer-item l6">
                <div class="layer-label">L6 — Tests</div>
                <div class="layer-content">
                    <strong>HOW</strong> do we verify correctness?<br>
                    <em>Example: "Test: expired token → 410 Gone"</em>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Why This Order?</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What It Determines</th><th>What It Constrains Below</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>L0 Business</td><td>Why we're building this</td><td>Scopes everything below</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L1 Functional</td><td>What users experience</td><td>Constrains architecture choices</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L2 Architecture</td><td>What components exist</td><td>Constrains interface design</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L3 Interfaces</td><td>How components talk</td><td>Constrains data model design</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L4 Data Models</td><td>What data is stored</td><td>Constrains logic implementation</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L5 Logic</td><td>How logic works</td><td>Constrains what tests are needed</td></tr>
                <tr><td>L6 Tests</td><td>How to verify correctness</td><td>Direct input to code generation</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            Dependencies are <strong>not only vertical</strong> (between layers). They can be
            <strong>horizontal</strong> (within a layer) and <strong>diagonal</strong> (skipping layers).
        </p>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 8: INTERACTION MODEL ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="interaction-model">
        <div class="section-number">Section 08</div>
        <h2>Interaction Model &amp; Conflict Philosophy</h2>

        <h3>The Write → Compile → Inspect Cycle</h3>

        <div class="flow">
            <div class="flow-step">✏️ Write Freely</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step" style="background: var(--accent-blue); color: var(--bg-primary); font-weight:700;">🔨 COMPILE</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">📊 See Graph</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">🔍 Inspect / Fix</div>
            <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
            <div class="flow-step">🔁 Repeat</div>
        </div>

        <h4>Step 1: Write Freely</h4>
        <p>User types specs in natural language. No vocabulary restrictions. Can write one sentence or a full paragraph. Can specify which layer they're targeting or let the system infer it.</p>

        <h4>Step 2: Compile (user explicitly triggers)</h4>
        <p>AI agent processes free-form text: extracts atomic requirements as new nodes, classifies each node into a formal layer, infers dependency edges to existing nodes, assigns to families, and runs conflict detection against the full graph.</p>

        <h4>Step 3: Feedback Display (immediate)</h4>
        <p>System shows new nodes created with layer assignments, edges inferred, and status indicators:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><span class="status-red">❌ CONFLICTS</span> highlighted (no fix suggested unless user asks)</li>
            <li><span class="status-yellow">⚠️ AMBIGUITIES</span> flagged (possible multiple interpretations noted)</li>
            <li><span class="status-green">✅ VALIDATED</span> nodes (consistent with graph)</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The Conflict Highlighting Philosophy</h3>

        <p>This encodes an important UX and workflow principle:</p>

        <div class="comparison">
            <div class="compare-box positive">
                <h4>✅ DO</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Highlight conflicting nodes immediately</li>
                    <li>Show WHY they conflict</li>
                    <li>Show the dependency path that creates the conflict</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box negative">
                <h4>❌ DON'T</h4>
                <ul>
                    <li>Auto-suggest fixes</li>
                    <li>Block the user from continuing</li>
                    <li>Require resolution before adding more specs</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p>
                <strong>Rationale:</strong> The user may have context the system doesn't.
                The user may be mid-thought and doesn't want interruption.
                Premature fix suggestions can anchor thinking in the wrong direction.
                The user explicitly asks for help when ready.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Design Assumptions</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Assumption</th><th>Description</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>#1 — Start from scratch</td><td>SDD specs are built from an empty graph</td></tr>
                <tr><td>#2 — Multi-person collaboration</td><td>Multiple people contribute to specs; coordination is a major requirement</td></tr>
                <tr><td>#3 — Code-only scope</td><td>This SDD-Compiler is for coding, not for business or general purposes</td></tr>
                <tr><td>#4 — Serial collaboration</td><td>For simplicity, all people work on the same version, each in series; versioning conflicts ignored for now</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 9: AI INTERNALS ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="ai-internals">
        <div class="section-number">Section 09</div>
        <h2>How AI Agents Actually Process Spec Comparison</h2>

        <p>
            When you ask an AI agent to compare two SDD spec documents and find inconsistencies,
            here's what actually happens inside the model — and why it fails.
        </p>

        <h3>The Cognitive Task</h3>

        <p>
            Finding an inconsistency requires the model to consider every statement in Spec A against
            every statement in Spec B — a <strong>quadratic comparison task</strong>. For 30 requirements
            in each spec, the model must implicitly evaluate <strong>900 pairs</strong>. Each pair requires
            semantic reasoning, not just string matching.
        </p>

        <pre>
Easy conflict (surface-level):
  S_a: "Reset token expires after 15 minutes"
  S_b: "Password recovery links should remain active for 24 hours"
  → Model must recognize these refer to the same concept with different values

Hard conflict (multi-hop reasoning):
  S_a: "System must support 10,000 concurrent users"
  S_b: "Each password reset generates a cryptographic token using 500ms of CPU time"
  → Model must calculate: 10,000 × 1% reset rate × 500ms = potential overload
  → Requires inference across statements that share NO keywords</pre>

        <h3>Known Failure Modes</h3>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <h4>Lost in the Middle</h4>
                <p>Research (Stanford/Berkeley, 2023) shows LLMs have <strong>degraded recall for information in the middle</strong> of long contexts. The model pays most attention to the beginning and end. Conflicts between the middle of Spec A and the beginning of Spec B are <strong>most likely to be missed</strong>. This is a systematic, architectural bias.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <h4>Attention Dilution at Scale</h4>
                <p>As the number of requirements grows, the model distributes attention across more pairs. Each individual comparison gets less attention, and <strong>accuracy degrades</strong>. Models are often good at finding 1–2 obvious conflicts but miss the 3rd, 4th, and 5th in the same document.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <h4>Hallucinated Conflicts</h4>
                <p>The model may invent fake conflicts due to: <strong>surface similarity traps</strong> (similar words ≠ actual conflict), <strong>over-inference</strong> (unwarranted logical leaps), or <strong>pleasing bias</strong> (you asked for conflicts, so it finds some even when there aren't any).</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-red);">
                <h4>No Verification Mechanism</h4>
                <p>When a compiler checks types, it can <strong>prove</strong> an error exists. When an LLM checks spec consistency, it produces a <strong>probabilistic guess</strong>. It's doing pattern matching on what "looks like" a conflict based on training data, not formal verification.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 10: GRAPH VALUE ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="graph-value">
        <div class="section-number">Section 10</div>
        <h2>Does the Graph Actually Help?</h2>

        <h3>Claim 1: Token Consumption</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Approach</th><th>Total Tokens</th><th>Accuracy</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Monolithic (paste both specs)</td><td>~8,500</td><td>Medium (attention dilution)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Graph-based (naive, every pair)</td><td>~25,000</td><td>High (focused comparisons)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Graph-based (smart filtering)</td><td>~17,500</td><td>High (focused + filtered)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <blockquote class="warning">
            <p>
                The graph trades tokens for accuracy.
                It's not about using <strong>fewer</strong> tokens.
                It's about using tokens <strong>more effectively</strong>.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Claim 2: Hallucination Reduction</h3>

        <div class="comparison">
            <div class="compare-box negative">
                <h4>Monolithic Comparison</h4>
                <p>"Find all conflicts in 6,000 words" = <strong>high ambiguity</strong>. Model must hold all info simultaneously, pressure to find something (pleasing bias), no structure to constrain reasoning. Output: "I found 7 conflicts" — some real, some hallucinated. <strong>You have no way to verify</strong> without reading both full specs yourself.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box positive">
                <h4>Graph-Based Comparison</h4>
                <p>"Do these two nodes conflict?" = <strong>low ambiguity</strong>. Much smaller input, binary question (harder to hallucinate), reasoning is <strong>auditable</strong>. If it says "conflict" you can read both nodes in 5 seconds and verify.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p>
                <strong>Key Insight:</strong> Hallucination is proportional to ambiguity of task.
                The graph <strong>decomposes</strong> a high-ambiguity task into many low-ambiguity tasks.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>Claim 3: Conflict Detection Accuracy</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Problem with Monolithic</th><th>How Graph Solves It</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Ordering Bias:</strong> Conflicts between paragraph 3 and paragraph 47 are less likely to be caught than between paragraphs 46 and 47</td>
                    <td>Each pair is presented in isolation. No ordering bias.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Semantic Distance:</strong> Requirements using different terminology for the same concept are hard to connect across a long document</td>
                    <td>Layer classification already identifies both as the same layer/family. They're presented together for comparison.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Multi-Hop Reasoning:</strong> Indirect conflicts require knowledge + inference, which is hard while juggling dozens of other statements</td>
                    <td>Model can focus ALL its reasoning capacity on one specific pair at a time.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Completeness:</strong> Monolithic produces a list with no guarantee of completeness. You can't know what was checked.</td>
                    <td>Graph provides an explicit record of which pairs were checked. Gaps are visible and auditable.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Honest Tradeoff Summary</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Monolithic</th><th>Graph-Based</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>Token cost</td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Lower</span></td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Higher</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Latency</td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Single call</span></td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Multiple calls</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Setup complexity</td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Zero</span></td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Need graph logic</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Hallucination rate</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Higher</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Lower</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Conflict accuracy</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Degrades with size</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Stable with size</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Completeness</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Unknown</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Auditable</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Verifiability</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Hard to check</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Easy to check</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Scalability</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Breaks at ~100 requirements</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Scales well</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Ordering bias</td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Significant</span></td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Eliminated</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Implementation effort</td><td><span class="status-green">✅ Trivial</span></td><td><span class="status-red">❌ Significant engineering</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>The Deepest Insight: The Graph Is for Humans, Not AI</h3>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p>
                <strong>Common assumption:</strong> "The graph helps the AI work better."<br><br>
                <strong>Deeper truth:</strong> "The graph helps <em>humans manage the AI's work</em>."<br><br>
                The graph is a <strong>coordination structure</strong>, not an AI optimization.
            </p>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The graph enables <strong>human oversight of the AI's reasoning process</strong>:</p>

        <div class="comparison">
            <div class="compare-box negative">
                <h4>Without Graph</h4>
                <p>"AI, compare these specs" → "I found 5 conflicts" → "Are you sure? Did you check everything?" → "I believe so" → <strong>No way to verify.</strong></p>
            </div>
            <div class="compare-box positive">
                <h4>With Graph</h4>
                <p>Each node is individually verifiable (5 seconds). Each comparison is logged and auditable. Structural visualization reveals gaps. <strong>You can challenge the AI on specific pairs it missed.</strong></p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <h3>The Graph Serves Three Purposes</h3>

        <div class="card-grid">
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-blue);">
                <h4>1. Decomposition for AI</h4>
                <p>Break a hard, open-ended task (compare two documents) into easy, closed-ended tasks (compare two statements). This is where accuracy and hallucination improvements come from.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-green);">
                <h4>2. Coordination for Humans</h4>
                <p>Give multiple people a shared, visual, inspectable representation of the spec state. This is where the collaboration value comes from.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="card" style="border-top: 3px solid var(--accent-purple);">
                <h4>3. Auditability for Trust</h4>
                <p>Create a verifiable record of what was checked, what conflicted, and what was resolved. This is where the "compiler" metaphor earns its name — compilers are trusted because their checks are systematic and complete.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <p>This is exactly how real compilers work:</p>

        <pre>
A compiler doesn't just say "your code is wrong."
It says:
  - file: auth.py, line: 47
  - error: type mismatch
  - expected: int, got: string
  - in function: validate_token()

The SDD-Compiler graph does the same for specifications:
  - node: F3
  - conflicts with: F7
  - property: token TTL
  - values: 15min vs 24hr
  - dependency impact: T1, T2 tests affected</pre>
    </div>

    <hr>

    <!-- ==================== SECTION 11: SIMILAR WORK ==================== -->
    <div class="section" id="similar-work">
        <div class="section-number">Section 11</div>
        <h2>Prior Art &amp; Similar Products</h2>

        <h3>Requirements Management Tools</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Similarity</th><th>Key Difference from SDD-Compiler</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>IBM DOORS</strong></td><td>Graph-based requirements traceability, layered linking</td><td>No AI, no free-form input, very manual node creation</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Jama Connect</strong></td><td>Traceability matrices, impact analysis</td><td>No AI, no conflict auto-detection, no layered graph</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Polarion (Siemens)</strong></td><td>Full ALM with requirements linked to tests and code</td><td>Enterprise-grade but no free-form-to-graph pipeline</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE)</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Tool / Method</th><th>Similarity</th><th>Key Difference</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>SysML / Capella</strong></td><td>Formal layered models: requirements → functional → logical → physical</td><td>Requires expertise in visual modeling language; no NL input</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>ARCADIA Method</strong></td><td>Formal layers: operational → system → logical → physical</td><td>Method for systems engineering, not software spec-to-code</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>AI-Powered Spec-to-Code Tools</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Similarity</th><th>Key Difference</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>MetaGPT</strong></td><td>Multi-agent pipeline: PRD → Design → Tasks → Code with formal layers</td><td>Fully automated — human doesn't interactively build graph. No conflict detection.</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>GPT Engineer</strong></td><td>Takes spec prompt, asks clarifying questions, generates code</td><td>Single-pass, no persistent graph, no multi-user</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Factory AI / Devin</strong></td><td>AI agent that plans and writes code from descriptions</td><td>Agent-oriented, no visible spec graph, no collaborative spec building</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Cursor / Windsurf</strong></td><td>IDE with AI that takes specs in context files</td><td>No graph structure, no conflict detection, single-user</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Formal Specification &amp; Constraint Systems</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Method / Tool</th><th>Similarity</th><th>Key Difference</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>Alloy Analyzer</strong></td><td>Builds a model, auto-finds constraint violations and counter-examples</td><td>Requires formal Alloy language, not natural language</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Specification by Example</strong> (Gojko Adzic)</td><td>Collaborative specification through concrete examples</td><td>Method, not a tool. No graph, no AI, no automation</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Event Storming</strong> (Alberto Brandolini)</td><td>Collaborative domain discovery, natural grouping into aggregates</td><td>Workshop method with physical sticky notes. SDD-Compiler is essentially a <strong>digital, AI-powered Event Storming</strong> with persistent graph structure</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Build Systems (The Explicit Analogy)</h3>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr><th>Tool</th><th>What SDD-Compiler Borrows</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><strong>CMake / Make</strong></td><td>Dependency graph construction before compilation, target-level nodes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Bazel</strong></td><td>Hermetic builds, strict layering, visibility rules between targets</td></tr>
                <tr><td><strong>Nx (Monorepo)</strong></td><td>Project graph with affected-target detection — knows what to rebuild when something changes</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3>Where SDD-Compiler Lives</h3>

        <blockquote class="insight">
            <p>
                <strong>No existing product combines all of these:</strong><br><br>
                MetaGPT's layered agent pipeline +<br>
                IBM DOORS' traceability graph +<br>
                Event Storming's collaborative discovery +<br>
                Alloy's automatic conflict detection +<br>
                CMake's dependency graph model<br><br>
                <strong>SDD-Compiler sits at the intersection of all five.</strong>
            </p>
        </blockquote>
    </div>

